{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0a912fc1126e901f0169bb1637625c8191a95137211b9c02b1", "created": 1773868599, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**KIDNEYS** are a pair of organs located in the abdomen behind other organs, with each kidney sitting beneath an adrenal gland (the adrenal glands rest on top of each kidney). They are major organs of the urinary system and urinary tract that filter the blood, remove waste products and excess water, help regulate fluid and chemical balance, and produce hormones. The kidneys make urine, which drains through the ureters to the bladder; after procedures such as cystectomy, urine may be routed out of the body via a urinary diversion.\n\nKidneys are frequently evaluated and monitored in cancer care and other serious illnesses because their function affects both safety and treatment choices. During treatments such as AML therapy and recovery, kidney health and function may be monitored through blood tests. Laboratory assessments commonly include blood chemistry testing such as a chemistry profile or comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP), which reflects chemical levels influenced by kidney function and often includes markers like blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine to assess how well the kidneys are filtering waste.\n\nKidney function can influence imaging decisions, particularly the use of contrast material; if the kidneys are not working well, clinicians may avoid contrast to reduce risk. The kidneys are also assessed with imaging tests of the urinary tract, including urograms, renal ultrasound, and pyelograms, to look for problems such as blockage or cancer spread. When abnormalities are suspected, the inner portions of the kidneys may also be examined during ureteroscopy. On PET imaging, the kidneys can normally appear “bright,” which is considered a typical finding.\n\nThe kidneys can be vulnerable to damage from systemic cancer therapies. They may be injured as a severe side effect of systemic treatment, including platinum-based chemotherapy agents (such as carboplatin, cisplatin, and oxaliplatin). Kidney function can determine whether certain drugs—particularly cisplatin—can be used and may require supportive measures such as IV hydration. The kidneys may also be harmed by high-dose methotrexate; in severe cases this can lead to kidney failure, so preventive strategies (often including hospital monitoring, IV fluids, and protective medications) may be used, and impaired kidney function may prompt clinicians to recommend alternative treatments.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 444, "prompt_tokens": 977, "total_tokens": 1421, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}